			"seed": 871977260,
			"version": 1526,
			"versionNonce": 228952745,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783437012307,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "1. Big-O Notation (O): The Upper Bound (Worst Case)\n    - Definition: It guarantees that the algorithm will never perform worse than a specified\n      limit.\n    - Why we use it: This is the industry and exam standard. We design systems around the \n      absolute worst-case scenario so they don't crash under peak load.\n2. Big-Omega Notation (Ω): The Lower Bound (Best Case)\n    - Definition: It describes the absolute best-case performance. Example: Linear search \n      finds the target item on the very first index of the array: Ω(1).\n3. Big-Theta Notation (θ): The Tight Bound (Average Case)\n    - Definition: If an algorithm's best case and worst case scale identically, θ provides a\n      precise, tight match.",
			"rawText": "1. Big-O Notation (O): The Upper Bound (Worst Case)\n    - Definition: It guarantees that the algorithm will never perform worse than a specified \n      limit.\n    - Why we use it: This is the industry and exam standard. We design systems around the \n      absolute worst-case scenario so they don't crash under peak load.\n2. Big-Omega Notation (Ω): The Lower Bound (Best Case)\n    - Definition: It describes the absolute best-case performance. Example: Linear search \n      finds the target item on the very first index of the array: Ω(1).\n3. Big-Theta Notation (θ): The Tight Bound (Average Case)\n    - Definition: If an algorithm's best case and worst case scale identically, θ provides a \n      precise, tight match.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "middle",
			"containerId": "DO7UPQ4JRsT_c2hOmmNg5",
			"originalText": "1. Big-O Notation (O): The Upper Bound (Worst Case)\n    - Definition: It guarantees that the algorithm will never perform worse than a specified \n      limit.\n    - Why we use it: This is the industry and exam standard. We design systems around the \n      absolute worst-case scenario so they don't crash under peak load.\n2. Big-Omega Notation (Ω): The Lower Bound (Best Case)\n    - Definition: It describes the absolute best-case performance. Example: Linear search \n      finds the target item on the very first index of the array: Ω(1).\n3. Big-Theta Notation (θ): The Tight Bound (Average Case)\n    - Definition: If an algorithm's best case and worst case scale identically, θ provides a \n      precise, tight match.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
