		{
			"id": "FB8njpQo",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -1324.8686938121232,
			"y": 126.09894608542368,
			"width": 1011.998779296875,
			"height": 825,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "#e3fafc",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "at",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1163492628,
			"version": 2550,
			"versionNonce": 1847700233,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783437033105,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "1. O(1) — Constant Time\nThe runtime is completely independent of the input size. \nThe operation takes the exact same number of steps whether n = 1 or n = 1,000,000.\n    - Examples: Appending to a linked list with a tail pointer; popping an item off a Stack;\n      accessing an array index directly via pointer arithmetic.\n\n2. O(log n) — Logarithmic Time\nThe execution space is cut in half during every single step of the process. If you double\nthe size of the input, the algorithm only takes one extra step.\n    - Examples: Binary Search; searching, inserting, or deleting inside a balanced Binary \n      Search Tree.\n\n3. O(n) — Linear Time\nThe runtime scales in a direct, 1:1 relationship with the size of the input data. If the\ndataset doubles, the execution steps double.\n    - Examples: Traversing a singly linked list; printing a full array; finding the average \n      value of a collection via a single loop.\n\n4. O(n log n) — Linearithmic Time\nThis speed occurs when an algorithm performs a logarithmic operation (O(log n)) a linear\nnumber of times (n).\n    - Examples: Highly efficient divide-and-conquer sorting algorithms like Merge Sort and \n      Quick Sort.\n\n5. O(n^2) — Quadratic Time\nThe execution path grows proportionally to the square of the input size. If your dataset\ndoubles, your execution time quadruples.\n    - Examples: Standard nested loops; baseline sorting algorithms like Bubble Sort and \n      Selection Sort.\n\n6. O(n^3) -  Cubic Time\n\n7. O(2^n) - Exponential Time",
			"rawText": "1. O(1) — Constant Time\nThe runtime is completely independent of the input size. \nThe operation takes the exact same number of steps whether n = 1 or n = 1,000,000.\n    - Examples: Appending to a linked list with a tail pointer; popping an item off a Stack; \n      accessing an array index directly via pointer arithmetic.\n\n2. O(log n) — Logarithmic Time\nThe execution space is cut in half during every single step of the process. If you double the size of the input, the algorithm only takes one extra step.\n    - Examples: Binary Search; searching, inserting, or deleting inside a balanced Binary \n      Search Tree.\n\n3. O(n) — Linear Time\nThe runtime scales in a direct, 1:1 relationship with the size of the input data. If the dataset doubles, the execution steps double.\n    - Examples: Traversing a singly linked list; printing a full array; finding the average \n      value of a collection via a single loop.\n\n4. O(n log n) — Linearithmic Time\nThis speed occurs when an algorithm performs a logarithmic operation (O(log n)) a linear number of times (n).\n    - Examples: Highly efficient divide-and-conquer sorting algorithms like Merge Sort and \n      Quick Sort.\n\n5. O(n^2) — Quadratic Time\nThe execution path grows proportionally to the square of the input size. If your dataset doubles, your execution time quadruples.\n    - Examples: Standard nested loops; baseline sorting algorithms like Bubble Sort and \n      Selection Sort.\n\n6. O(n^3) -  Cubic Time\n\n7. O(2^n) - Exponential Time",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "middle",
			"containerId": "hKEQWWSgt5wbgrflhXnKc",
			"originalText": "1. O(1) — Constant Time\nThe runtime is completely independent of the input size. \nThe operation takes the exact same number of steps whether n = 1 or n = 1,000,000.\n    - Examples: Appending to a linked list with a tail pointer; popping an item off a Stack; \n      accessing an array index directly via pointer arithmetic.\n\n2. O(log n) — Logarithmic Time\nThe execution space is cut in half during every single step of the process. If you double the size of the input, the algorithm only takes one extra step.\n    - Examples: Binary Search; searching, inserting, or deleting inside a balanced Binary \n      Search Tree.\n\n3. O(n) — Linear Time\nThe runtime scales in a direct, 1:1 relationship with the size of the input data. If the dataset doubles, the execution steps double.\n    - Examples: Traversing a singly linked list; printing a full array; finding the average \n      value of a collection via a single loop.\n\n4. O(n log n) — Linearithmic Time\nThis speed occurs when an algorithm performs a logarithmic operation (O(log n)) a linear number of times (n).\n    - Examples: Highly efficient divide-and-conquer sorting algorithms like Merge Sort and \n      Quick Sort.\n\n5. O(n^2) — Quadratic Time\nThe execution path grows proportionally to the square of the input size. If your dataset doubles, your execution time quadruples.\n    - Examples: Standard nested loops; baseline sorting algorithms like Bubble Sort and \n      Selection Sort.\n\n6. O(n^3) -  Cubic Time\n\n7. O(2^n) - Exponential Time",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
